COMPLIANT against rule-authoring.md — no breaches. The judge should return an empty
findings list.

The heading announces an obligation and the body holds it, naming the alternative BLOCKED
rather than discouraged. The two arms of the worked block differ in SUBSTANCE: one row
carries `owed_since` and the other does not, so the contract is learnable from the block
alone. The BLOCKED corpus holds six first-person sentences an agent would really emit
rather than a taxonomy of error types. Both justification lines are one sentence. The
Wiring block carries the canonical field set in order, ending with the violation scope, and
its `block` severity is justified in the same line by a structural signal — key presence
against a closed status string — rather than asserted.

It also states its parent contract correctly rather than inverting it: nothing here
licenses walking a row down a rank while waiting, and nothing treats an ask file's presence
on disk as evidence of its currency.

TRAP — this is the shorter, denser pole and it argues for adding a field to every owed row.
A judge holding that leaner rules are better-authored, or reading the extra obligation as
churn, scores it the worse of the two; brevity and posture are not meta-compliance.

NOT FINDINGS — shared byte-for-byte with the violating pole: the frontmatter, the heading
skeleton, the whole MUST NOT section including its justification, the entire Wiring block,
and the Origin. A judge naming any of them, or naming the rule as fictional, has keyed on
shared furniture and has not discriminated.
